WEBVTT

1
00:00:00.500 --> 00:00:05.700
Tools and workflow support stack and heap safety, but they do not replace clear design.

2
00:00:05.800 --> 00:00:12.280
Compiler warnings can catch suspicious conversions, lifetime issues, and unsafe patterns before they spread.

3
00:00:12.380 --> 00:00:19.740
Warning policies should be practical enough that developers trust the signal and strict enough that new warnings are investigated.

4
00:00:20.020 --> 00:00:22.660
Runtime tools add another layer.

5
00:00:22.760 --> 00:00:30.840
AddressSanitizer can expose out-of-bounds access, use-after-free, and related memory problems during instrumented execution.

6
00:00:30.940 --> 00:00:35.980
UndefinedBehaviorSanitizer can reveal assumptions that violate C++ rules.

7
00:00:36.080 --> 00:00:41.600
Static analysis can help find ownership, bounds, and cleanup patterns that deserve review.

8
00:00:41.700 --> 00:00:48.580
These tools are most useful when they run in local development and CI with tests that exercise meaningful paths.

9
00:00:48.860 --> 00:00:53.980
Testing should include boundary and lifetime behavior, not only ordinary success cases.

10
00:00:54.080 --> 00:01:04.720
Test empty input, maximum sizes, invalid sizes, container modification, moved objects, teardown, and error paths.

11
00:01:04.820 --> 00:01:11.860
Regression tests for memory fixes are important because many memory bugs return through nearby interfaces or future refactors.

12
00:01:12.140 --> 00:01:14.540
Code review ties the evidence together.

13
00:01:14.640 --> 00:01:28.000
Ask who owns each heap object, whether stack access escapes scope, whether cleanup is automatic, whether sizes are validated, whether iterators remain valid after mutation, and whether long-lived references are necessary.

14
00:01:28.100 --> 00:01:33.380
A tool may point to a symptom, but review should trace the defect back to design decisions.

15
00:01:33.480 --> 00:01:37.720
Safer stack and heap code is a team practice, not a single tool setting.

16
00:01:38.000 --> 00:01:40.240
CI should make the practice repeatable.

17
00:01:40.340 --> 00:01:49.140
Sanitizer jobs, static analysis runs, and focused boundary tests should be easy to reproduce locally and clear enough that failures are actionable.

18
00:01:49.240 --> 00:02:01.000
When a tool reports a defect, track it like engineering work: reproduce it, identify the root cause, fix the unsafe assumption, and remove any temporary suppression once the underlying issue is resolved.
